WEBVTT

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.980
Chief! Chief! The cloud outside
my balcony keeps growing taller!

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.540
It was all white, and now
its bottom's turned grey.

00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:23.977
Better bring the laundry in soon.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.030
But I don't get it. Is there
really water in a cloud?

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:38.100
Yes, lots. An ordinary cumulus cloud
holds about 500 tonnes of water.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.000
You've seen the answer every
summer. Nobody told you, that's all.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.500
First, a guess: 500 tonnes
is how many elephants?

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.650
And will it fall on you as rain? How soon?

00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:57.000
Isn't water heavier than air?

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.440
So why doesn't the whole thing fall down?

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.390
This morning, that cloud didn't exist.
The sky was clear, not one cloud.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.980
The sun heats the ground; the ground
heats the layer of air touching it.

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:17.887
So where do clouds come
from? Is it water vapour?

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.750
Many people think so. But look at this.

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:27.313
When a kettle boils, the bit
right by the spout is see-through.

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:32.047
The white mist only shows
up a little way out.

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:37.237
So the white stuff isn't water vapour?

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:40.943
Right. Real water vapour is invisible.

00:01:40.943 --> 00:01:45.083
The white is vapour cooled
back into tiny drops.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:52.030
Clouds are the same: lots of tiny drops,
sometimes tiny ice crystals, not a gas.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.940
But there's no kettle in
the sky. What cools it down?

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:04.050
It climbs. Sun-warmed air is lighter than
the air around it, so it rises in bubbles.

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:07.223
Like a hot-air balloon?

00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:12.237
Sort of. And you've felt it: the higher
you climb a mountain, the cooler it gets.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:19.787
Rising air meets lower pressure, so
it expands; and expanding cools it.

00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:27.733
Before it condenses, rising air cools
about 10 °C for every kilometre it climbs.

00:02:27.733 --> 00:02:32.393
At some height the air is saturated;
the extra vapour condenses.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.030
Like the drops on a cold
drink's cup in summer.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.220
Drops stick to the cup. What
do they stick to up there?

00:02:43.233 --> 00:02:49.643
Good question. Air is full of specks
you can't see: dust, sea salt, smoke.

00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:54.937
The vapour condenses on
those specks into tiny drops.

00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:58.943
So cumulus clouds often have flat bases:

00:02:58.943 --> 00:03:02.727
that height is where air
gets cold enough to condense.

00:03:04.233 --> 00:03:08.313
It's true! They all have
the same flat bottoms!

00:03:09.233 --> 00:03:12.140
While warm air keeps coming up from below,

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.783
the cloud keeps growing,
like a cauliflower.

00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:20.073
How do you get 500 tonnes?

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.014
Let's work it out for an
ordinary cumulus cloud.

00:03:24.014 --> 00:03:28.350
Say it's a big box, one
kilometre long, wide and high.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.230
The water in a cloud is thin:
about half a gram per cubic metre.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.870
But a cubic kilometre is
a billion cubic metres.

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:44.617
A billion times half a
gram... how much is that?

00:03:44.733 --> 00:03:46.843
The 500 tonnes from the start.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.900
About as heavy as a hundred elephants.

00:03:51.967 --> 00:03:55.587
That many elephants
floating over my house?!

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.740
Sounds scary, right? One might
be over your house right now.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.667
Wait, air weighs something too?

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:11.400
Yes, even more. That same cubic kilometre
of air weighs about a million tonnes.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.433
So the water is just a tiny
bit mixed into the air?

00:04:16.433 --> 00:04:19.354
Right. And the water isn't one big lump.

00:04:19.354 --> 00:04:22.533
It's split into a huge
number of tiny bits.

00:04:22.533 --> 00:04:27.221
A typical cloud droplet is about two
hundredths of a millimetre across,

00:04:27.221 --> 00:04:29.613
several times thinner than a hair.

00:04:31.367 --> 00:04:35.167
So it's so small it
doesn't weigh anything?

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:42.500
It does have weight, and it really
is falling. Just very, very slowly.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:46.920
Think: drop a stone and a feather
together. Which lands first?

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.530
The stone!

00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:54.933
The smaller something is, the more
the air holds it back, for its size.

00:04:54.933 --> 00:04:57.833
Droplet-sized, the air almost holds it up.

00:04:57.833 --> 00:05:03.633
In still air, a cloud droplet
falls only about 1 cm per second.

00:05:03.633 --> 00:05:06.253
That's way slower than walking!

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:13.563
At that speed, falling from a cloud
base 1 km up would take over a day.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.750
And it dries up on the
way before it lands.

00:05:17.833 --> 00:05:22.993
But the cloud stays all
afternoon. It doesn't slowly sink.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:28.510
Because the air in that cloud isn't still.
Remember the warm air that keeps rising?

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.500
In a growing cumulus cloud, the rising
air can move several metres a second.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:41.240
So it's riding an elevator up!

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:46.441
Exactly. A cloud has weight, but
its water is in countless bits,

00:05:46.441 --> 00:05:49.327
each light enough for air to hold.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:54.333
How big must a drop get to be too heavy?

00:05:54.333 --> 00:06:00.103
Raindrops you've felt are often about
2 mm: 100 times a droplet's width.

00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:03.567
Only 100? That's not much.

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:08.007
That's the width. For volume, you
multiply in all three directions.

00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:15.227
A hundred times a hundred,
times a hundred, is a million.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.090
One raindrop needs that many?!

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:20.627
Roughly that many.

00:06:21.667 --> 00:06:24.977
How do they get together?
Does someone squish them?

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.900
Nobody squishes them. They bump together.

00:06:30.933 --> 00:06:33.297
Cloud drops come in many sizes.

00:06:33.297 --> 00:06:38.023
Big ones fall faster, so they
catch and hit smaller ones below.

00:06:38.867 --> 00:06:43.367
They stick, it gets bigger,
falls faster, hits more.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.020
Like a snowball!

00:06:46.033 --> 00:06:50.913
Just like that. It's called collision
and coalescence. But that's only one way.

00:06:52.833 --> 00:06:59.133
High enough up, a cloud gets below
0 °C. There are tiny ice crystals,

00:06:59.133 --> 00:07:03.900
and drops colder than 0 °C
that still aren't frozen.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.130
Below zero and not frozen?

00:07:07.333 --> 00:07:09.575
Yes, that's common in clouds.

00:07:09.575 --> 00:07:13.853
And there, ice crystals grab
vapour better than drops do.

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:20.827
The crystals grow as they fall, then melt
into rain in the warmer air lower down.

00:07:22.067 --> 00:07:25.767
So my rain might have been snow?

00:07:25.767 --> 00:07:30.497
It might. In tall summer storm
clouds, both ways can happen.

00:07:32.567 --> 00:07:35.897
Either way, the drops must
grow big enough first.

00:07:37.533 --> 00:07:41.113
Once they're big, does
the whole cloud fall?

00:07:41.933 --> 00:07:44.913
When a cloud rains,
it's still there, right?

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:47.650
...Oh, yeah.

00:07:47.933 --> 00:07:50.313
What falls is the big drops.

00:07:50.767 --> 00:07:54.377
Too heavy for the rising air,
they fall out the cloud base.

00:07:56.167 --> 00:08:00.059
Raindrops like these can
fall 6 to 7 m a second,

00:08:00.059 --> 00:08:02.567
hundreds of times faster
than cloud droplets.

00:08:02.567 --> 00:08:07.237
The cloud stays put. Only the
grown drops leave, batch by batch.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:13.200
But near my house, some
clouds rain, some never do.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.967
Why doesn't every cloud rain?

00:08:15.967 --> 00:08:19.397
Back to the start: how
tall the cloud grows,

00:08:19.397 --> 00:08:22.377
and how hard the air inside rushes up.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.037
The small, flat white clouds
of fine days can't grow tall,

00:08:27.037 --> 00:08:29.330
so they fade before their drops grow.

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:35.533
Heavy rain comes from clouds
that shoot all the way up.

00:08:35.533 --> 00:08:40.367
Their tops pass 10 km, as
high as airliners or higher.

00:08:40.367 --> 00:08:44.287
That high! That needs lots of warm air.

00:08:44.733 --> 00:08:48.583
Yes. So guess: when is the
ground hottest in a day?

00:08:49.967 --> 00:08:51.477
Twelve noon!

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.686
Lots of people guess that.

00:08:53.686 --> 00:08:58.620
The sun is highest at noon, but the
ground and air get hottest later.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:04.550
Like a pan on the stove: it isn't
hottest right away. It takes a while.

00:09:06.667 --> 00:09:10.697
So warm air often rises
hardest in the afternoon.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.700
Why so many in Taiwan?

00:09:14.767 --> 00:09:19.827
Taiwan's summer has two more helpers:
damp air blowing in from the sea,

00:09:21.833 --> 00:09:27.083
and mountains. Slopes push air up,
so clouds grow easily by mountains.

00:09:28.433 --> 00:09:34.493
So at three in the afternoon, that
cloud outside my house... turned grey!

00:09:35.133 --> 00:09:40.773
Yes. It's so tall and thick that sunlight
can't pass, so its base looks grey.

00:09:41.733 --> 00:09:45.963
So why do thunderstorms
often stop so soon?

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:49.490
Because a big rain turns
off its own engine.

00:09:50.067 --> 00:09:52.825
Falling rain drags the air around it down;

00:09:52.825 --> 00:09:56.587
rain drying up on the way
makes that air even colder.

00:09:58.267 --> 00:10:02.717
The cool wind before a storm is
often this air hitting the ground.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.640
I know that wind! Then it rains!

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:11.767
Cold air pours down and
blocks the warm air.

00:10:11.767 --> 00:10:15.667
With no warm air coming in, the
cloud slowly runs out of steam.

00:10:15.667 --> 00:10:18.500
Remember when you asked how long it takes?

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:23.260
A single storm cloud often grows
and fades away in under an hour.

00:10:23.833 --> 00:10:26.403
But it's not like this every afternoon.

00:10:28.133 --> 00:10:32.046
How damp the air is,
how cold it is up high,

00:10:32.046 --> 00:10:36.383
how the wind blows: if one is
wrong, the cloud can't grow.

00:10:36.767 --> 00:10:40.597
So how do I know if
today's clouds will rain?

00:10:41.133 --> 00:10:44.623
Chief! Its top is still bubbling up!

00:10:44.733 --> 00:10:49.500
Remember the cauliflower? It means
the air inside is still rushing up.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.100
Just look up to see it.

00:10:51.867 --> 00:10:55.577
Now the top's gone flat
and it's spreading out!

00:10:56.367 --> 00:11:00.837
Like an anvil. It's grown up to
a layer it can hardly get past.

00:11:02.933 --> 00:11:07.500
Whoa, it's suddenly so
cool! The laundry's flying!

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:11.567
Remember that cool wind? The cold air
is already running ahead of the rain.

00:11:11.567 --> 00:11:13.600
What did I say at the start?

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:15.567
Laundry!

00:11:15.567 --> 00:11:20.667
Big drops! Are raindrops
pointy, like tears?

00:11:20.667 --> 00:11:23.333
Everyone draws that. No.

00:11:23.333 --> 00:11:27.833
The faster a drop falls,
the harder air pushes up.

00:11:27.833 --> 00:11:34.293
Small ones are nearly round. Bigger ones
get flat bottoms, like a hamburger bun.

00:11:35.367 --> 00:11:40.627
Any bigger and they're torn into
pieces. So raindrops can't grow forever.

00:11:42.067 --> 00:11:46.407
So what hit my hand was a hamburger bun!

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:52.240
Laundry's in, and the rain
stopped. Under an hour!

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:54.850
Where did that cloud go?

00:11:55.967 --> 00:11:58.733
The engine's off, so the cloud fades away.

00:11:58.733 --> 00:12:03.253
Every drop that hit your hand was
about a million droplets put together.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:09.600
Remember those tiny drops? It's not that
they don't fall. They're waiting to grow!

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.530
So that's why!

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.726
When do afternoon thunderstorms
usually start where you live?

00:12:14.726 --> 00:12:16.500
Tell us in the comments.

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:21.400
Want to get to the bottom of everyday
whys? Subscribe to So That's Why.
